##  Purpose and Motivation
Trajopt is a tool to help users design control *strategies* for aircraft with complex dynamics. Using the aerosanbox interface with CASADi, Trajopt abstrats away most of the problem setup to create *trajectory optimization* problems using aircraft dynamics as the constraint. what is a trajectory optimizaiton problem?



The motivaiton for Trajopt is the development of control strategies for limiting the landing dispersion of blown-lift aircraft subject to gusts on approach. The reason for this is that 1) blown lift aircraft using electric drivetrains are *overactuated* systems that have multiple control inputs in $u$ which affect the same dynamic axis, 2) the aerodynamics of blowing is extremely nonlinear 3) blowing aircraft have non-unique conbinations of $x,u$ which can develop the same $\dot{x}$. S


## why use trajectory optimizaiton over other control generation strategies? 

## What it is and what it is not
It is worth restating from above that trajopt is specifically not designed to build controllers, but designs entire trajectories in x,u which optimize some policy over an entire problemm. So, while a controller will decide at time t some optimal policy u(t) some immediate cost, this problem formulation looks over an entire interval. It may more accurately be lumped in planning, or model predictive control, directly a controller. 

As will be dicussed later, this does not specifically prevent Trajopt's use for looking at optimizing a control policy over a trajectory which may then later be implemented. But it does mean that it's use is specifically not as a controller-in-the-loop




# Format of Actual Update
Intended to show a knowledgeable user how to construct a trajectory optimizaiton problem using trajopt. The intent is by the end of the explanation the user could build their own trajectory problem based on the example (in this case landing with a single gust)

